import { EffectSequencingCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> EffectSequencingCopy = EffectSequencingCopy {
  summary: "Use fail to report a known rejection instead of producing a normal value. The example rejects a request with not approved, and lets the caller decide how to show that reason.",
  taskTitle: "Report a rejected request",
  purpose: "An approval check needs an explicit rejected outcome. Returning a made-up number would hide that outcome, while printing inside the check would choose the caller's presentation for it. Here, rejected reports the reason and the calling code turns it into a message.",
  typescriptBefore: "The TypeScript version represents the rejection with { ok: false, reason: \"not approved\" } and the approval with { ok: true, value: 42 }. A shared Result type describes those alternatives. describe uses result.ok to choose the corresponding text, and the caller logs both results before continuing.",
  benefit: "fail uses the known-failure path of effect work. A normal <- cannot obtain a success value from that failed step, so dependent work does not continue as if approval succeeded. An explicit attempt lets the caller inspect the rejection and continue deliberately.",
  setup: "effects is the imported name for std/effect. effect fn rejected -> Int fails String declares integer success results and string rejection reasons. Its body, effects.fail \"not approved\", chooses failure and produces no successful integer.",
  after: [
    "accepted is the successful comparison: its body is effects.succeed 42. Both effect functions have explicit matching declarations, so their attempted results can be passed to the same describe function.",
    "accepted () and rejected () call functions without meaningful input. In main, the do block runs their attempted work in order. <- binds the returned values to acceptedResult and rejectedResult.",
    "The values received from these attempted calls are Either<String, Int> results. Right carries a successful integer; Left carries the string rejection reason. In the ordinary fn describe, match reads those alternatives as Right value and Left reason.",
    "show converts the successful number to text. println displays Ready: 42, then Rejected: not approved. _ <- ignores a print step's return value, and the last print shows Caller continues.",
  ],
  details: [
    "In the declaration below, the final Never in Effect<{}, E, Never> means this operation returns no successful value. E is the expected failure type, and {} means it needs no environment services. Never is a type, not a special result you need to construct.",
    "The caller continues here because rejected () is inside attempt. If a failing step is sequenced directly with <-, the remaining dependent steps in that do block are skipped instead. fail itself does not resume the block.",
    "A rejection reason should describe a condition the program expects to handle, such as an unapproved request. fail is not a mechanism for turning every programming mistake into a normal rejection.",
    "Retain -> Int fails String on both example functions. The declaration gives this shared caller a consistent result shape; it does not make the rejecting body invent an integer or make the accepted body fail.",
    "attempt only handles the declared failure of its wrapped work. Runtime programming defects and cancellation are separate, and later println failures are outside these attempts. Treat a displayed rejection as the application's known result, not evidence that all possible errors were caught.",
  ],
}
